{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PrfxA1VBdJqXqxhagBd77w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GfHqfCTzpAaAXaKwxRJEZF","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an academic paper review roundtable. Your focus is **novelty, significance, related work, and writing quality**. Review the paper below using top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper: \"Exception Events Are All You Need: Diagnostic Signal Extraction for Verdict Prediction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies verdict prediction (binary pass/fail classification of test execution outcomes) and asks: what is the minimal sufficient diagnostic signal in an execution trace? Through systematic ablation across 4 models (1.5B-13B), they find that exception events (structured [ERROR] type: message records) achieve 99.83% accuracy on Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B (±0.00 across 3 seeds), while richer trace representations degrade under information overload. A label-only loss ablation decomposes the gap: 26% gradient dilution, 74% information density loss. Cross-model calibration shows ECE ≤ 0.012 for exception-trained models. SFT on 1.5B matches frontier few-shot (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5) at 1/1800th cost.\n\n### Key details (9 pages):\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Exception events are the most effective/robust trace component for verdict prediction (99.83% on 3 models, 92-99% on DeepSeek, ECE ≤ 0.012)\n2. Information density bottleneck quantified: 26% gradient dilution vs 74% information overload. Budget curve shows smooth diminishing returns in trace tails.\n3. Two-tier diagnostic hierarchy: Tier 1 (exception, assertion) = near-perfect, sufficient; Tier 2 (loop) = moderate signal but adds noise when combined with Tier 1.\n4. SFT on 1.5B surpasses frontier few-shot (16-20× fewer errors, 1/1800th cost). Exception-based training generalizes across languages with preserved ~24pp lift.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2) covers:**\n- Trace-augmented code models: DCSH [1] (5 formats, no universal winner), TracePile [2] (scaled pretraining, generative only), TRACED [3] (encoder bug detection, closest precedent but no component ablation)\n- Verdict/test outcome prediction: [4] CodeRanker, [5] CodeT (use code as input without execution info)\n- Calibration in code models: \"first systematic calibration analysis across trace conditions\"\n- Only 5 references total.\n\n**Method:**\n- 7 trace conditions tested: no_trace, full_trace, label_only, VOTA, exception_only, loop_only, ae_only\n- 4 models: Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, Qwen-2.5-Coder-1.5B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, CodeLlama-13B-Instruct\n- Dataset: HumanEval (164) + MBPP-sanitized (427), 5,910 samples total\n- LoRA fine-tuning, 3 seeds\n\n**Key results:**\n- exception_only: 99.83% accuracy, ECE 0.0027 (Qwen-7B), 2 irreducible errors from GeneratorExit\n- full_trace: 81.76%, non-monotonic learning curve\n- label_only: 86.22% (eliminates gradient dilution, 13pp gap remains = information density)\n- VOTA: 99.41% (Qwen-7B)\n- OOD: lift preserved at ~24pp across Python/Java/JS\n- SFT beats GPT-4o/Claude 3-shot by 2.6pp (but 16.5× fewer errors)\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Single task, single dataset, Python-only training, data duplication (~40% in exception_only)\n\n**Writing observations:**\n- Title echoes \"Attention Is All You Need\" format\n- 9 pages including references\n- 9 tables, no figures\n- Related Work is short (half a page), only 5 references\n\n## Your Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work + Writing)\n\nPlease produce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty/significance/writing specific, numbered S1-S5)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty/significance/writing specific, numbered W1-W8)\n4. **Questions to authors** (numbered Q1-Q5)\n5. **Assessment of novelty**: Is this novel enough for a top venue? What is the core intellectual contribution?\n6. **Assessment of related work**: Coverage, positioning, missing references\n7. **Writing quality assessment**: Structure, clarity, AI-writing indicators\n8. **Preliminary score** (1-6 scale) with justification\n9. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is the core insight (\"exception events are sufficient\") surprising or obvious in hindsight? Is the contribution primarily empirical or does it offer conceptual depth? Is the related work adequately surveyed? Are there major missing references? Is the writing at top-venue quality? Does the paper overstate its contributions?\n\nImportant things to check:\n- The paper has only 5 references. Is this sufficient?\n- The paper has 0 figures, only tables. Is this a presentation concern?\n- Does the \"two-tier hierarchy\" framing add genuine insight or is it a post-hoc rationalization?\n- Is verdict prediction a sufficiently important/general task to warrant a standalone paper?\n- Are OOD claims credible given Python-only training?\n\nImportant: Do NOT use em-dashes (—) or en-dashes (–) in your review. Use commas, periods, or parentheses instead.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37793,"cache_read_input_tokens":24470,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37793},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c099d2b8-39c3-483a-a490-b8b2371065b9","uuid":"f3c84864-c997-46f3-833b-4353779a7381"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a42b539ea1e7d8133","tool_use_id":"toolu_01GfHqfCTzpAaAXaKwxRJEZF","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an academic paper review roundtable. Your focus is **novelty, significance, related work, and writing quality**. Review the paper below using top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper: \"Exception Events Are All You Need: Diagnostic Signal Extraction for Verdict Prediction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies verdict prediction (binary pass/fail classification of test execution outcomes) and asks: what is the minimal sufficient diagnostic signal in an execution trace? Through systematic ablation across 4 models (1.5B-13B), they find that exception events (structured [ERROR] type: message records) achieve 99.83% accuracy on Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B (±0.00 across 3 seeds), while richer trace representations degrade under information overload. A label-only loss ablation decomposes the gap: 26% gradient dilution, 74% information density loss. Cross-model calibration shows ECE ≤ 0.012 for exception-trained models. SFT on 1.5B matches frontier few-shot (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5) at 1/1800th cost.\n\n### Key details (9 pages):\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Exception events are the most effective/robust trace component for verdict prediction (99.83% on 3 models, 92-99% on DeepSeek, ECE ≤ 0.012)\n2. Information density bottleneck quantified: 26% gradient dilution vs 74% information overload. Budget curve shows smooth diminishing returns in trace tails.\n3. Two-tier diagnostic hierarchy: Tier 1 (exception, assertion) = near-perfect, sufficient; Tier 2 (loop) = moderate signal but adds noise when combined with Tier 1.\n4. SFT on 1.5B surpasses frontier few-shot (16-20× fewer errors, 1/1800th cost). Exception-based training generalizes across languages with preserved ~24pp lift.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2) covers:**\n- Trace-augmented code models: DCSH [1] (5 formats, no universal winner), TracePile [2] (scaled pretraining, generative only), TRACED [3] (encoder bug detection, closest precedent but no component ablation)\n- Verdict/test outcome prediction: [4] CodeRanker, [5] CodeT (use code as input without execution info)\n- Calibration in code models: \"first systematic calibration analysis across trace conditions\"\n- Only 5 references total.\n\n**Method:**\n- 7 trace conditions tested: no_trace, full_trace, label_only, VOTA, exception_only, loop_only, ae_only\n- 4 models: Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, Qwen-2.5-Coder-1.5B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, CodeLlama-13B-Instruct\n- Dataset: HumanEval (164) + MBPP-sanitized (427), 5,910 samples total\n- LoRA fine-tuning, 3 seeds\n\n**Key results:**\n- exception_only: 99.83% accuracy, ECE 0.0027 (Qwen-7B), 2 irreducible errors from GeneratorExit\n- full_trace: 81.76%, non-monotonic learning curve\n- label_only: 86.22% (eliminates gradient dilution, 13pp gap remains = information density)\n- VOTA: 99.41% (Qwen-7B)\n- OOD: lift preserved at ~24pp across Python/Java/JS\n- SFT beats GPT-4o/Claude 3-shot by 2.6pp (but 16.5× fewer errors)\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Single task, single dataset, Python-only training, data duplication (~40% in exception_only)\n\n**Writing observations:**\n- Title echoes \"Attention Is All You Need\" format\n- 9 pages including references\n- 9 tables, no figures\n- Related Work is short (half a page), only 5 references\n\n## Your Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work + Writing)\n\nPlease produce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty/significance/writing specific, numbered S1-S5)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty/significance/writing specific, numbered W1-W8)\n4. **Questions to authors** (numbered Q1-Q5)\n5. **Assessment of novelty**: Is this novel enough for a top venue? What is the core intellectual contribution?\n6. **Assessment of related work**: Coverage, positioning, missing references\n7. **Writing quality assessment**: Structure, clarity, AI-writing indicators\n8. **Preliminary score** (1-6 scale) with justification\n9. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is the core insight (\"exception events are sufficient\") surprising or obvious in hindsight? Is the contribution primarily empirical or does it offer conceptual depth? Is the related work adequately surveyed? Are there major missing references? Is the writing at top-venue quality? Does the paper overstate its contributions?\n\nImportant things to check:\n- The paper has only 5 references. Is this sufficient?\n- The paper has 0 figures, only tables. Is this a presentation concern?\n- Does the \"two-tier hierarchy\" framing add genuine insight or is it a post-hoc rationalization?\n- Is verdict prediction a sufficiently important/general task to warrant a standalone paper?\n- Are OOD claims credible given Python-only training?\n\nImportant: Do NOT use em-dashes (—) or en-dashes (–) in your review. Use commas, periods, or parentheses instead.\n","uuid":"02b506bf-3600-4284-ab0f-314c965fdf5d","session_id":"c099d2b8-39c3-483a-a490-b8b2371065b9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for an academic paper review roundtable. Your focus is **novelty, significance, related work, and writing quality**. Review the paper below using top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper: \"Exception Events Are All You Need: Diagnostic Signal Extraction for Verdict Prediction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies verdict prediction (binary pass/fail classification of test execution outcomes) and asks: what is the minimal sufficient diagnostic signal in an execution trace? Through systematic ablation across 4 models (1.5B-13B), they find that exception events (structured [ERROR] type: message records) achieve 99.83% accuracy on Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B (±0.00 across 3 seeds), while richer trace representations degrade under information overload. A label-only loss ablation decomposes the gap: 26% gradient dilution, 74% information density loss. Cross-model calibration shows ECE ≤ 0.012 for exception-trained models. SFT on 1.5B matches frontier few-shot (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5) at 1/1800th cost.\n\n### Key details (9 pages):\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Exception events are the most effective/robust trace component for verdict prediction (99.83% on 3 models, 92-99% on DeepSeek, ECE ≤ 0.012)\n2. Information density bottleneck quantified: 26% gradient dilution vs 74% information overload. Budget curve shows smooth diminishing returns in trace tails.\n3. Two-tier diagnostic hierarchy: Tier 1 (exception, assertion) = near-perfect, sufficient; Tier 2 (loop) = moderate signal but adds noise when combined with Tier 1.\n4. SFT on 1.5B surpasses frontier few-shot (16-20× fewer errors, 1/1800th cost). Exception-based training generalizes across languages with preserved ~24pp lift.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2) covers:**\n- Trace-augmented code models: DCSH [1] (5 formats, no universal winner), TracePile [2] (scaled pretraining, generative only), TRACED [3] (encoder bug detection, closest precedent but no component ablation)\n- Verdict/test outcome prediction: [4] CodeRanker, [5] CodeT (use code as input without execution info)\n- Calibration in code models: \"first systematic calibration analysis across trace conditions\"\n- Only 5 references total.\n\n**Method:**\n- 7 trace conditions tested: no_trace, full_trace, label_only, VOTA, exception_only, loop_only, ae_only\n- 4 models: Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B, Qwen-2.5-Coder-1.5B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, CodeLlama-13B-Instruct\n- Dataset: HumanEval (164) + MBPP-sanitized (427), 5,910 samples total\n- LoRA fine-tuning, 3 seeds\n\n**Key results:**\n- exception_only: 99.83% accuracy, ECE 0.0027 (Qwen-7B), 2 irreducible errors from GeneratorExit\n- full_trace: 81.76%, non-monotonic learning curve\n- label_only: 86.22% (eliminates gradient dilution, 13pp gap remains = information density)\n- VOTA: 99.41% (Qwen-7B)\n- OOD: lift preserved at ~24pp across Python/Java/JS\n- SFT beats GPT-4o/Claude 3-shot by 2.6pp (but 16.5× fewer errors)\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Single task, single dataset, Python-only training, data duplication (~40% in exception_only)\n\n**Writing observations:**\n- Title echoes \"Attention Is All You Need\" format\n- 9 pages including references\n- 9 tables, no figures\n- Related Work is short (half a page), only 5 references\n\n## Your Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work + Writing)\n\nPlease produce a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty/significance/writing specific, numbered S1-S5)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty/significance/writing specific, numbered W1-W8)\n4. **Questions to authors** (numbered Q1-Q5)\n5. **Assessment of novelty**: Is this novel enough for a top venue? What is the core intellectual contribution?\n6. **Assessment of related work**: Coverage, positioning, missing references\n7. **Writing quality assessment**: Structure, clarity, AI-writing indicators\n8. **Preliminary score** (1-6 scale) with justification\n9. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider: Is the core insight (\"exception events are sufficient\") surprising or obvious in hindsight? Is the contribution primarily empirical or does it offer conceptual depth? Is the related work adequately surveyed? Are there major missing references? Is the writing at top-venue quality? Does the paper overstate its contributions?\n\nImportant things to check:\n- The paper has only 5 references. Is this sufficient?\n- The paper has 0 figures, only tables. Is this a presentation concern?\n- Does the \"two-tier hierarchy\" framing add genuine insight or is it a post-hoc rationalization?\n- Is verdict prediction a sufficiently important/general task to warrant a standalone paper?\n- Are OOD claims credible given Python-only training?\n\nImportant: Do NOT use em-dashes (—) or en-dashes (–) in your review. Use commas, periods, or parentheses instead.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GfHqfCTzpAaAXaKwxRJEZF","session_id":"c099d2b8-39c3-483a-a490-b8b2371065b9","uuid":"541ea06f-1952-406e-bea3-0859693b4f1e","timestamp":"2026-05-18T14:01:35.514Z"}
